 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection algorithm in MATLAB, you need to add an extra step to your existing code. Here's how you can modify it:
```MATLAB
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Apply Gaussian filter to reduce noise
gaussian_filter = fspecial('gaussian', [3,3], 0.5); % Create a 3x3 Gaussian filter with standard deviation of 0.5
smoothed_image = imfilter(gray_image, gaussian_filter); % Apply the filter to the grayscale image
% Define Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(smoothed_image), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(smoothed_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display original image, smoothed image, and edge image
subplot(1,3,1), imshow(input_image); title('Original Image');
subplot(1,3,2), imshow(smoothed_image); title('Smoothed Image');
subplot(1,3,3), imshow(edge_image); title('Edge Image');
```
This modified code first applies a Gaussian filter using the `fspecial` function to create a 3x3 filter and the `imfilter` function to apply it to the grayscale image. The resulting smoothed image is then used as the input for the Sobel edge detection algorithm. This helps to reduce the noise in the image and improve the quality of the edge detection results.

