 To retrieve the username from a JWT (JSON Web Token) in an ASP.NET C# WebApi project, you can follow these steps:

1. First, make sure that you have added the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` NuGet package to your project. This package contains the necessary classes for handling JWT tokens in ASP.NET.

2. Create a method in your controller or extension class that extracts the username from the JWT token. Here's an example of how to implement this method as an extension:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http;

public static string GetUsernameFromJwtToken(this HttpRequest request)
{
    var authHeader = request.Headers["Authorization"].FirstOrDefault()?.ToString();
    if (authHeader == null || !authHeader.StartsWith("Bearer "))
        throw new ArgumentException("Invalid Authorization header.");

    var token = authHeader.Split(' ').Last();
    var handler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();

    try
    {
        var jwtToken = handler.ReadJwtToken(token);
        return jwtToken.Claims.FirstOrDefault(x => x.Type == ClaimTypes.Name)?.Value;
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        throw new Exception($"Failed to parse JWT token: {ex.Message}");
    }
}
```

3. Now you can use the extension method in your action methods like this:

```csharp
[ApiController]
[Route("[controller]")]
public class ValuesController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet]
    public ActionResult<string> Get([FromServices] IMyService myService)
    {
        var username = Request.GetUsernameFromJwtToken();
        return Ok(username);
    }
}
```

This example assumes you have an `IMyService` dependency injected into your controller. Replace it with your own service if needed. The `GetUsernameFromJwtToken` method will return the username claim value from the JWT token present in the Authorization header.

